      "locked": false,
      "text": "# Data Structure and Algorithm – Short Notes\n\n## 1. Data Structure\n\nA **Data Structure** is a way of **organizing and storing data** so that it can be accessed and modified efficiently.\n\n**Examples:**\n\n* Array\n* Linked List\n* Stack\n* Queue\n* Tree\n* Graph\n* Hash Table\n\n**Example:** A Queue stores elements using **FIFO (First In, First Out)**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Algorithm\n\nAn **Algorithm** is a **step-by-step procedure or set of instructions** used to solve a problem or perform a specific task.\n\n**Examples:**\n\n* Linear Search\n* Binary Search\n* Bubble Sort\n* Merge Sort\n* Quick Sort\n* BFS\n* DFS\n\n**Example:** Binary Search is an algorithm used to find an element efficiently in a sorted array.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Difference Between Data Structure and Algorithm\n\n| Data Structure                           | Algorithm                                  |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| Organizes and stores data                | Solves a problem using a sequence of steps |\n| Focuses on how data is stored            | Focuses on how data is processed           |\n| Examples: Array, Stack, Tree             | Examples: Binary Search, Quick Sort        |\n| Helps manage and access data efficiently | Helps solve problems efficiently           |\n\n### Easy Way to Remember\n\n**Data Structure = How/Where data is stored**\n\n**Algorithm = Steps used to solve a problem**\n\n**Example:**\n**Bookshelf → Data Structure**\n**Steps to find a particular book → Algorithm**\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "# Data Structure and Algorithm – Short Notes\n\n## 1. Data Structure\n\nA **Data Structure** is a way of **organizing and storing data** so that it can be accessed and modified efficiently.\n\n**Examples:**\n\n* Array\n* Linked List\n* Stack\n* Queue\n* Tree\n* Graph\n* Hash Table\n\n**Example:** A Queue stores elements using **FIFO (First In, First Out)**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Algorithm\n\nAn **Algorithm** is a **step-by-step procedure or set of instructions** used to solve a problem or perform a specific task.\n\n**Examples:**\n\n* Linear Search\n* Binary Search\n* Bubble Sort\n* Merge Sort\n* Quick Sort\n* BFS\n* DFS\n\n**Example:** Binary Search is an algorithm used to find an element efficiently in a sorted array.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Difference Between Data Structure and Algorithm\n\n| Data Structure                           | Algorithm                                  |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| Organizes and stores data                | Solves a problem using a sequence of steps |\n| Focuses on how data is stored            | Focuses on how data is processed           |\n| Examples: Array, Stack, Tree             | Examples: Binary Search, Quick Sort        |\n| Helps manage and access data efficiently | Helps solve problems efficiently           |\n\n### Easy Way to Remember\n\n**Data Structure = How/Where data is stored**\n\n**Algorithm = Steps used to solve a problem**\n\n**Example:**\n**Bookshelf → Data Structure**\n**Steps to find a particular book → Algorithm**\n",
